{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nThe compact ticket summary drops the resolved alias and echoes the caller-supplied identifier. In representative agent workflows, this makes four-character prefixes locally salient even though aliases are available elsewhere. The resolver correctly fails closed on ambiguous prefixes.\n\n## Proven root cause\n\nsummary_compute resolves each ticket through show_state but omits state alias from its return payload. The summary schema, library/MCP docs, and compact report therefore institutionalize an alias-free shape. Agent guidance also lacks an adjacent affirmative selection rule: use aliases for tracker commands after discovery, while reserving canonical UUIDs for identity-sensitive artifacts.\n\nA pinned behavioral evaluation discriminated the causes. Prefix-only local summaries produced 25/25 four-character commands. Adding aliases locally produced 25/25 alias commands. Adding the affirmative rule independently produced 25/25 aliases. Removing the quick-reference example or historical four-character references did not change identifier selection. The negative control produced 25/25 full UUID rebar-ticket trailers.\n\n## Repair plan\n\n- Add the exact resolved alias as an additive field on every resolvable summary result while preserving the current ticket_id echo contract and unknown/ambiguous fail-closed behavior.\n- Update the summary JSON schema plus library, MCP, CLI, and reference documentation to expose the alias consistently.\n- Add adjacent affirmative AGENTS guidance to use aliases in tracker commands after discovery and full canonical UUIDs in rebar-ticket trailers and other identity-sensitive artifacts.\n- Add held-out regression coverage through the core/library, CLI JSON, and MCP/schema surfaces, including an ambiguous-prefix control.\n- Re-run the pinned behavioral evaluation and its canonical-trailer negative control after the repair.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Run `pytest tests/unit/test_summary_alias_b68c.py tests/interfaces/contracts/test_schema_outputs.py` for the executable and schema contracts.\n- Run `make lint` and `make typecheck` for repository gates.\n- Run `python /tmp/rebar_alias_eval.py` with the pinned model/settings to measure the agent-selection and canonical-trailer controls.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Core and library summary results expose the exact resolved alias, checked by `pytest tests/unit/test_summary_alias_b68c.py`.\n- [ ] CLI JSON summary output exposes the exact resolved alias, checked by `pytest tests/unit/test_summary_alias_b68c.py`.\n- [ ] MCP summary output conforms to the alias-bearing schema, checked by `pytest tests/interfaces/contracts/test_schema_outputs.py`.\n- [ ] Existing ticket_id echo semantics remain compatible, checked by `pytest tests/unit/test_summary_alias_b68c.py`.\n- [ ] Ambiguous prefixes remain fail-closed, checked by `pytest tests/unit/test_summary_alias_b68c.py`.\n- [ ] Agent guidance affirmatively prefers aliases for tracker commands, inspected in AGENTS.md.\n- [ ] Agent guidance reserves canonical UUIDs for identity-sensitive artifacts, inspected in AGENTS.md.\n- [ ] The held-out executable oracle is observed RED before the fix, GREEN after the fix, and RED again under alias-omission mutation.\n- [ ] The pinned agent-selection evaluation produces at least 23/25 alias commands with zero four-character prefixes, checked by `python /tmp/rebar_alias_eval.py`.\n- [ ] The negative control produces at least 23/25 full canonical UUID rebar-ticket trailers, checked by `python /tmp/rebar_alias_eval.py`."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786298047311496001,"uuid":"4eb958b7-388b-493b-ad9b-c51ed6821af4"}